WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.834
Module Three, Lesson Three: prompting, RAG, or fine-tuning.

2
00:00:04.164 --> 00:00:06.717
It's the exam's most frequent decision question.

3
00:00:07.517 --> 00:00:10.841
Picture a bank's support assistant that quotes last week's

4
00:00:10.891 --> 00:00:13.867
savings rates and ignores the required reply format.

5
00:00:14.197 --> 00:00:17.005
The team's instinct is to fine-tune on everything.

6
00:00:17.805 --> 00:00:21.425
But the rule is: RAG for knowledge, fine-tuning for behavior.

7
00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:24.243
Prompting covers everything you can get away with.

8
00:00:25.043 --> 00:00:30.135
Use RAG when facts change, answers need citations, or access varies by user.

9
00:00:30.465 --> 00:00:34.191
Knowledge can be added or revoked instantly, with no retraining.

10
00:00:34.991 --> 00:00:39.271
Use fine-tuning for a consistent format, tone, or tool-call syntax.

11
00:00:39.601 --> 00:00:42.965
It can even make a small model match a big one on a narrow task.

12
00:00:43.765 --> 00:00:48.024
So the bank retrieves live rates, and fine-tunes a small model for its format.

13
00:00:48.354 --> 00:00:51.781
Together they give large-model quality at small-model cost.

14
00:00:52.581 --> 00:00:55.795
Full fine-tuning updates every weight, and costs the most.

15
00:00:56.125 --> 00:00:59.425
LoRA trains just a tenth of a percent to one percent of them.

16
00:01:00.225 --> 00:01:04.697
LoRA freezes the base weights, so one deployed model can serve many adapters.

17
00:01:05.027 --> 00:01:09.969
NIM supports this: a custom variant per customer, on a tight GPU budget.

18
00:01:10.769 --> 00:01:13.876
DPO learns from chosen and rejected pairs,

19
00:01:13.926 --> 00:01:17.183
and RFT from a verifier, like passing tests.

20
00:01:17.513 --> 00:01:21.133
Distillation shrinks a large model's behavior into a small one.

21
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:23.632
Data quality beats quantity.

22
00:01:23.962 --> 00:01:28.422
A thousand clean examples routinely beat a hundred thousand scraped ones,

23
00:01:28.472 --> 00:01:30.461
which is why NeMo Curator exists.

24
00:01:31.261 --> 00:01:35.819
Beware catastrophic forgetting, where narrow fine-tuning breaks unrelated skills.

25
00:01:36.149 --> 00:01:40.622
Prefer LoRA, mix in general data, and evaluate on a broad held-out suite.

26
00:01:41.422 --> 00:01:44.241
NVIDIA's data flywheel loops through capture,

27
00:01:44.291 --> 00:01:46.791
curate, customize, evaluate, and deploy.

28
00:01:47.121 --> 00:01:50.108
The payoff is a small model, tuned on real traffic,

29
00:01:50.158 --> 00:01:52.788
matching a big one at a fraction of the cost.

30
00:01:53.588 --> 00:01:56.244
The classic trap: a bot quotes outdated prices,

31
00:01:56.294 --> 00:01:58.893
so should you fine-tune on the new price list?

32
00:01:59.223 --> 00:02:01.366
No: fine-tuning teaches how to answer,

33
00:02:01.416 --> 00:02:04.079
while retrieval supplies what's true right now.

34
00:02:04.879 --> 00:02:07.773
That one rule wins the exam's most common question.

35
00:02:08.103 --> 00:02:11.467
Read on for the full decision table and every tuning method.
